  _isSmallIngredient: 0
  _recipeBookDescriptionUnspoiled: A breakfast staple for all ages, beloved due to
    its portability and versatility. Anything in season can be used as fillings to
    make this sweet and light pastry. When apples flood the market tables, apple
    kolaches would be seen in the hands of teenagers strutting up the streets. During
    the hot summer months, apricot kolaches would decorate the bakeries in the City
    Center. Pear was a popular variant during the cold winter months. Nowadays, only
    hardy, borderline unspoilable cheese can be found in enough abundance for a lowly
    cook like you to put into your kolaches.
  _recipeBookDescriptionSpoiled: There's not exactly a conventional word to describe
    this dish, but it tastes like a kolache's death. It tastes like the dough remembers
    the feeling of its body being crushed, milled, beaten and burnt. It tastes like
    the cheese was made from milk that squeezed out the last life essence of the
    poor, poor cow, groaning in agony as its soul empties out into a bucket. It tastes
    like the oven was angry about being beaten against its will into a machine of
    flame, its rage searing into every molecule of the pastry. It tastes like the
    pain, the disgust, the hate from the cook was distilled and each and every bite
    of this pastry was given the sole purpose of inflicting all of this to another
    person.
